1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,231
On the western edge of San
Francisco there is a four

2
00:00:04,231 --> 00:00:08,077
lane highway that the city
is tearing out on purpose.

3
00:00:08,077 --> 00:00:11,923
It runs along Ocean Beach,
past rows of pastel houses

4
00:00:11,923 --> 00:00:15,769
that sell for two, three, and
sometimes four million dollars.

5
00:00:15,769 --> 00:00:19,615
The bulldozers are not coming
because the road wore out.

6
00:00:19,615 --> 00:00:22,692
They are coming because
the city decided the

7
00:00:22,692 --> 00:00:25,385
Pacific Ocean had
already won the argument.

8
00:00:25,385 --> 00:00:29,231
So why would a city
give up its own coastal

9
00:00:29,231 --> 00:00:33,077
road while the houses behind
it keep getting more expensive?

10
00:00:33,077 --> 00:00:35,000
The road opened in 1929.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,231
In the same years the city
poured a concrete seawall along

12
00:00:39,231 --> 00:00:43,077
the beach and built a boardwalk
lined with amusement rides.

13
00:00:43,077 --> 00:00:46,154
Back then the sand dunes
still rolled unbroken

14
00:00:46,154 --> 00:00:49,231
from the beach almost to Golden Gate Park.

15
00:00:49,231 --> 00:00:52,692
The road was meant to be a scenic drive.

16
00:00:52,692 --> 00:00:56,154
Families took a Sunday
car out to the water

17
00:00:56,154 --> 00:00:59,615
and watched the fog come in over the hood.

18
00:00:59,615 --> 00:01:03,846
For nearly a hundred years
that is exactly what it was.

19
00:01:03,846 --> 00:01:08,077
Today the southern stretch is closed
to cars behind orange barriers.

20
00:01:08,077 --> 00:01:11,538
The middle stretch has
been turned into a park.

21
00:01:11,538 --> 00:01:14,615
And the sand keeps
arriving on the pavement

22
00:01:14,615 --> 00:01:17,692
faster than the plows can push it back.

23
00:01:17,692 --> 00:01:22,308
The homeowners along the road
did not vote for most of this.

24
00:01:22,308 --> 00:01:26,154
Yet the value of their houses is tied to a

25
00:01:26,154 --> 00:01:29,615
strip of asphalt that
no longer has a future.

26
00:01:29,615 --> 00:01:35,385
To understand how a city loses a
highway, you have to start with the sand.

27
00:01:35,385 --> 00:01:39,231
Ocean Beach is roughly
three and a half miles long.

28
00:01:39,231 --> 00:01:42,692
It is the widest beach
in San Francisco and

29
00:01:42,692 --> 00:01:46,154
the only one that faces
the open Pacific directly.

30
00:01:46,154 --> 00:01:50,385
The waves here are born in
storms thousands of miles away.

31
00:01:50,385 --> 00:01:55,000
In winter they break at fifteen
and twenty feet, and the surfers

32
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,231
who paddle out are among the
most experienced in the state.

33
00:01:59,231 --> 00:02:01,923
The wind blows onshore
almost every afternoon.

34
00:02:01,923 --> 00:02:05,385
It carries fine dune
sand east across the road

35
00:02:05,385 --> 00:02:08,846
in drifts that can
close a lane by morning.

36
00:02:08,846 --> 00:02:14,231
City crews have plowed that sand for
decades the way northern towns plow snow.

37
00:02:14,231 --> 00:02:20,385
Front loaders scoop it off the lanes and
dump trucks carry it back to the beach.

38
00:02:20,385 --> 00:02:25,385
On the worst days the road closed
entirely until the sand was cleared.

39
00:02:25,385 --> 00:02:28,846
Anyone who has driven
the road in a westerly

40
00:02:28,846 --> 00:02:32,308
gale remembers the sound of
grit against the windshield.

41
00:02:32,308 --> 00:02:34,615
That sand is the beach moving.

42
00:02:34,615 --> 00:02:38,077
It shifts north in summer
and south in winter.

43
00:02:38,077 --> 00:02:41,923
Over the long run it has
been leaving the southern

44
00:02:41,923 --> 00:02:45,385
end of the beach faster
than it comes back.

45
00:02:45,385 --> 00:02:50,000
The southern end is where the
story of the road actually begins.

46
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,615
South of Sloat Boulevard the highway
does not sit behind a seawall.

47
00:02:54,615 --> 00:03:00,000
It sits on top of a sandy bluff
about forty feet above the water.

48
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
The bluff is made of old dune sand
and decades of construction fill.

49
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:09,615
Beneath it runs one of the most
important pipes in the city.

50
00:03:09,615 --> 00:03:14,231
The Lake Merced Tunnel is a concrete
sewer line fourteen feet across.

51
00:03:14,231 --> 00:03:18,077
It carries stormwater and
wastewater from the west side of

52
00:03:18,077 --> 00:03:21,923
San Francisco to the Oceanside
treatment plant beside the zoo.

53
00:03:21,923 --> 00:03:26,154
When it rains hard, that
tunnel is what keeps sewage out

54
00:03:26,154 --> 00:03:30,385
of the ocean and off the
streets of the Sunset District.

55
00:03:30,385 --> 00:03:35,385
The plant next to it handles a large
share of the city's wastewater.

56
00:03:35,385 --> 00:03:40,385
So the question in front of the
city was bigger than a road.

57
00:03:40,385 --> 00:03:44,615
It was about whether a forty
foot pile of sand could

58
00:03:44,615 --> 00:03:48,846
keep protecting a tunnel, a plant,
and a neighborhood behind them.

59
00:03:48,846 --> 00:03:52,692
The winter that answered the
question came in early 2010.

60
00:03:52,692 --> 00:03:55,769
A strong El Nino season sent one storm

61
00:03:55,769 --> 00:03:58,846
after another into the
coast of central California.

62
00:03:58,846 --> 00:04:03,846
At Ocean Beach the waves cut straight
into the bluff south of Sloat.

63
00:04:03,846 --> 00:04:07,308
In a matter of weeks the edge of the

64
00:04:07,308 --> 00:04:10,769
cliff moved back by roughly
forty feet in places.

65
00:04:10,769 --> 00:04:13,846
Sections of the parking
lots along the highway

66
00:04:13,846 --> 00:04:16,538
broke off and dropped onto the sand.

67
00:04:16,538 --> 00:04:19,615
The southbound lanes were
closed as the pavement

68
00:04:19,615 --> 00:04:22,692
cracked within a few yards of the drop.

69
00:04:22,692 --> 00:04:28,462
City engineers stood at the edge and
looked at the raw face of the bluff.

70
00:04:28,462 --> 00:04:33,077
The sewer tunnel was closer to
daylight than anyone had planned for.

71
00:04:33,077 --> 00:04:36,154
What happened next is the same move you

72
00:04:36,154 --> 00:04:38,846
see on every eroding
coastline in California.

73
00:04:38,846 --> 00:04:40,385
Trucks brought in boulders.

74
00:04:40,385 --> 00:04:44,615
Thousands of tons of rock
were pushed over the edge and

75
00:04:44,615 --> 00:04:48,846
stacked against the base of the
bluff as an emergency revetment.

76
00:04:48,846 --> 00:04:52,308
Old concrete rubble that
had been buried in the

77
00:04:52,308 --> 00:04:55,385
fill for decades tumbled
out onto the beach.

78
00:04:55,385 --> 00:05:01,538
Chunks of curb and slab lay in the surf
with rusted rebar sticking out of them.

79
00:05:01,538 --> 00:05:06,154
Surfers and dog walkers picked their
way around the debris for years.

80
00:05:06,154 --> 00:05:08,462
The California Coastal
Commission regulates almost

81
00:05:08,462 --> 00:05:10,385
everything built along the shoreline.

82
00:05:10,385 --> 00:05:13,462
It allowed the rock as
a temporary measure.

83
00:05:13,462 --> 00:05:18,462
Temporary is a word that does
a lot of work on this coast.

84
00:05:18,462 --> 00:05:22,308
Fifteen years later much of
that rock is still there.

85
00:05:22,308 --> 00:05:28,462
The rock protected the tunnel, but it
did nothing for the beach in front of it.

86
00:05:28,462 --> 00:05:32,308
Along a hard revetment the
sand tends to scour away.

87
00:05:32,308 --> 00:05:38,462
At high tide the water reaches the
boulders with no dry sand left to walk on.

88
00:05:38,462 --> 00:05:41,923
The structure that saved
the road was slowly erasing

89
00:05:41,923 --> 00:05:45,000
the beach the road was built to enjoy.

90
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,077
For a city that sells itself on its

91
00:05:48,077 --> 00:05:50,769
coastline, that trade
looked worse every winter.

92
00:05:50,769 --> 00:05:55,385
Photographs from the years after 2010
show tumbled rock and broken asphalt.

93
00:05:55,385 --> 00:06:00,000
A chain link fence stands
where a parking lot used to be.

94
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:03,077
The storms did not stop after 2010 either.

95
00:06:03,077 --> 00:06:06,154
In January 2023 a string
of atmospheric river

96
00:06:06,154 --> 00:06:09,231
storms hit the California
coast for three weeks.

97
00:06:09,231 --> 00:06:13,077
At Sloat the waves once
again reached the base of

98
00:06:13,077 --> 00:06:16,923
the bluff and pulled sand
out from under the revetment.

99
00:06:16,923 --> 00:06:20,385
Crews came back with more
rock and more trucks.

100
00:06:20,385 --> 00:06:22,692
Each repair bought another few winters.

101
00:06:22,692 --> 00:06:25,385
Nobody involved described
it as a solution.

102
00:06:25,385 --> 00:06:28,077
In 2012 a planning
organization called SPUR

103
00:06:28,077 --> 00:06:30,385
published the Ocean Beach Master Plan.

104
00:06:30,385 --> 00:06:34,231
It was not a law, only a
set of recommendations.

105
00:06:34,231 --> 00:06:37,692
But it laid out an idea that almost no

106
00:06:37,692 --> 00:06:41,154
American city had been
willing to say out loud.

107
00:06:41,154 --> 00:06:45,385
South of Sloat, the plan said,
the city should stop fighting.

108
00:06:45,385 --> 00:06:47,692
Remove the highway from that stretch.

109
00:06:47,692 --> 00:06:50,385
Reroute traffic onto Sloat
and Skyline boulevards.

110
00:06:50,385 --> 00:06:55,000
Protect the sewer tunnel with a
low wall buried under a restored

111
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:59,231
beach instead of a pile of
boulders standing in the surf.

112
00:06:59,231 --> 00:07:01,154
Planners call this managed retreat.

113
00:07:01,154 --> 00:07:05,000
Residents along the coast usually
call it something less polite.

114
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,077
The city adopted the core of that idea.

115
00:07:08,077 --> 00:07:11,923
Under the name Ocean Beach Climate
Change Adaptation Project, San

116
00:07:11,923 --> 00:07:15,385
Francisco committed to removing
the roadway south of Sloat.

117
00:07:15,385 --> 00:07:20,385
The plan calls for a buried seawall
to shield the Lake Merced Tunnel.

118
00:07:20,385 --> 00:07:26,923
New sand goes on top of it, and a coastal
trail runs where the cars used to.

119
00:07:26,923 --> 00:07:30,385
It moved through years of
environmental review and won

120
00:07:30,385 --> 00:07:33,462
its Coastal Commission
approval in the early 2020s.

121
00:07:33,462 --> 00:07:37,692
Construction was expected to run
through the middle of the decade.

122
00:07:37,692 --> 00:07:40,000
Cost estimates have shifted several times.

123
00:07:40,000 --> 00:07:44,231
I have not found a single
final figure I would trust,

124
00:07:44,231 --> 00:07:48,077
but every version runs well past
one hundred million dollars.

125
00:07:48,077 --> 00:07:51,154
The southern lanes were
closed to traffic ahead

126
00:07:51,154 --> 00:07:54,231
of the demolition, and
the barriers went up.

127
00:07:54,231 --> 00:07:58,462
That was the part of the
road the ocean took directly.

128
00:07:58,462 --> 00:08:01,538
The next part was taken by a ballot.

129
00:08:01,538 --> 00:08:05,000
Between Sloat Boulevard and
Lincoln Way, the upper highway

130
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:08,462
runs about two miles behind
the old 1929 seawall.

131
00:08:08,462 --> 00:08:11,923
Here the problem is sand
piling up rather than

132
00:08:11,923 --> 00:08:15,385
sand disappearing, and the road
itself was structurally fine.

133
00:08:15,385 --> 00:08:19,231
In the spring of 2020,
during the pandemic, the city

134
00:08:19,231 --> 00:08:23,077
closed it to cars so people
had somewhere to walk.

135
00:08:23,077 --> 00:08:25,385
Joggers, cyclists, and
parents with strollers

136
00:08:25,385 --> 00:08:27,692
filled four lanes of empty asphalt.

137
00:08:27,692 --> 00:08:31,538
The closure was popular with
families from across the city.

138
00:08:31,538 --> 00:08:36,154
It was deeply unpopular with many
of the people who actually lived

139
00:08:36,154 --> 00:08:40,385
in the Sunset and used
the road to get to work.

140
00:08:40,385 --> 00:08:43,077
For four years the city ran compromises.

141
00:08:43,077 --> 00:08:45,385
Cars on weekdays, pedestrians on weekends.

142
00:08:45,385 --> 00:08:48,462
Then in November 2024 the question went to

143
00:08:48,462 --> 00:08:51,538
every voter in San
Francisco as Proposition K.

144
00:08:51,538 --> 00:08:55,385
Proposition K asked whether the
upper highway should be closed

145
00:08:55,385 --> 00:08:58,846
to private vehicles permanently
and converted into a park.

146
00:08:58,846 --> 00:09:02,692
It passed with roughly fifty
five percent of the vote.

147
00:09:02,692 --> 00:09:07,308
The map of that vote tells the
whole story of the neighborhood.

148
00:09:07,308 --> 00:09:10,769
Precincts in the Sunset and Richmond
districts, the neighborhoods

149
00:09:10,769 --> 00:09:13,846
closest to the beach,
voted heavily against it.

150
00:09:13,846 --> 00:09:17,308
The eastern side of the
city carried the measure.

151
00:09:17,308 --> 00:09:21,154
Those are residents who visit
the beach on weekends and

152
00:09:21,154 --> 00:09:24,615
never sit in the commute
traffic on Nineteenth Avenue.

153
00:09:24,615 --> 00:09:28,846
In April 2025 the city opened
the stretch as Sunset Dunes.

154
00:09:28,846 --> 00:09:32,692
It is a linear park with
painted murals on the

155
00:09:32,692 --> 00:09:36,154
asphalt, a skate spot, and
benches facing the water.

156
00:09:36,154 --> 00:09:41,923
On a weekend afternoon thousands of people
walk, roll, and ride along the old lanes.

157
00:09:41,923 --> 00:09:44,231
Food trucks park at the entrances.

158
00:09:44,231 --> 00:09:48,077
For the people who use it, the park is one

159
00:09:48,077 --> 00:09:51,923
of the best things the
city has built in years.

160
00:09:51,923 --> 00:09:56,923
For the people who live on the row,
the picture is more complicated.

161
00:09:56,923 --> 00:10:01,154
Their driveways now open onto
a park instead of a road.

162
00:10:01,154 --> 00:10:03,846
Deliveries, contractors, and
moving trucks squeeze onto

163
00:10:03,846 --> 00:10:06,538
the narrow side streets behind the houses.

164
00:10:06,538 --> 00:10:10,000
Emergency vehicles have a
longer route to the beach.

165
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:14,231
And the plows that once kept
the sand off the pavement

166
00:10:14,231 --> 00:10:18,462
come far less often, because
there are no cars to protect.

167
00:10:18,462 --> 00:10:21,538
The political bill came
due five months later.

168
00:10:21,538 --> 00:10:25,769
Joel Engardio was the district supervisor
who had championed the measure.

169
00:10:25,769 --> 00:10:29,231
In September 2025 he faced
a recall election driven

170
00:10:29,231 --> 00:10:32,308
largely by residents angry
about losing the road.

171
00:10:32,308 --> 00:10:34,231
He was removed from office.

172
00:10:34,231 --> 00:10:39,231
A supervisor recalled over a highway
closure is rare anywhere in the country.

173
00:10:39,231 --> 00:10:45,385
It shows how much that strip of pavement
meant to the people who lived along it.

174
00:10:45,385 --> 00:10:48,846
Traffic on the parallel streets,
Sunset Boulevard and Nineteenth

175
00:10:48,846 --> 00:10:51,923
Avenue, became a daily argument
at neighborhood meetings.

176
00:10:51,923 --> 00:10:55,385
Whatever you think of the
outcome, the residents were

177
00:10:55,385 --> 00:10:58,846
right that something had
been taken away from them.

178
00:10:58,846 --> 00:11:05,000
Now walk the length of the road as it
stands today and look at the houses.

179
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:07,308
That is where the money is.

180
00:11:07,308 --> 00:11:10,769
The row of homes directly
facing the highway was

181
00:11:10,769 --> 00:11:13,846
mostly built between
the 1920s and the 1950s.

182
00:11:13,846 --> 00:11:18,077
They are two and three story
stucco houses with garages on

183
00:11:18,077 --> 00:11:21,923
the ground floor and living
rooms upstairs for the view.

184
00:11:21,923 --> 00:11:26,154
In the Outer Sunset the median
home has been selling around

185
00:11:26,154 --> 00:11:30,385
one and a half to two million
dollars in recent years.

186
00:11:30,385 --> 00:11:33,846
The oceanfront row along the
highway commands more, and

187
00:11:33,846 --> 00:11:36,923
individual sales there have
cleared three million dollars.

188
00:11:36,923 --> 00:11:41,538
Real estate listings describe the location
with words like unobstructed and forever.

189
00:11:41,538 --> 00:11:45,769
The photographs are always taken
on the rare clear afternoon, with

190
00:11:45,769 --> 00:11:50,000
the sun setting into the water
and the fog held offshore.

191
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:54,615
Buyers from the tech companies downtown
have paid cash for these houses.

192
00:11:54,615 --> 00:12:00,000
Some of them moved in without ever
spending a winter week on the block.

193
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:02,692
On paper these are among the most

194
00:12:02,692 --> 00:12:05,385
desirable addresses in
a very expensive city.

195
00:12:05,385 --> 00:12:08,846
In practice their owners
deal with a set of

196
00:12:08,846 --> 00:12:12,308
problems that do not show
up in listing photographs.

197
00:12:12,308 --> 00:12:14,231
The first problem is sand.

198
00:12:14,231 --> 00:12:18,846
Every westerly wind moves the dunes
in front of the seawall eastward.

199
00:12:18,846 --> 00:12:21,923
The road was the buffer that caught it.

200
00:12:21,923 --> 00:12:25,385
With the cars gone, the
city no longer plows

201
00:12:25,385 --> 00:12:28,462
the upper highway the way it used to.

202
00:12:28,462 --> 00:12:33,462
The drifts creep across the old lanes
toward the sidewalks and garage doors.

203
00:12:33,462 --> 00:12:36,538
Homeowners along the
row sweep sand off their

204
00:12:36,538 --> 00:12:39,615
front steps the way other
people sweep leaves.

205
00:12:39,615 --> 00:12:43,077
Salt spray pits the paint
on cars parked outside.

206
00:12:43,077 --> 00:12:47,308
It corrodes window frames and
door hinges within a few years.

207
00:12:47,308 --> 00:12:51,154
Fog sits over the neighborhood
for much of the summer.

208
00:12:51,154 --> 00:12:55,000
The sunshine that the rest of
California takes for granted

209
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,462
often does not appear on
this block until afternoon.

210
00:12:58,462 --> 00:13:00,385
The second problem is water.

211
00:13:00,385 --> 00:13:06,154
Over the life of a thirty year mortgage
it is the one that matters most.

212
00:13:06,154 --> 00:13:08,077
The 1929 seawall is aging.

213
00:13:08,077 --> 00:13:11,538
It was designed under Michael
O'Shaughnessy, the city engineer

214
00:13:11,538 --> 00:13:14,615
who also built the Hetch
Hetchy water system.

215
00:13:14,615 --> 00:13:17,692
His crews poured a
curved concrete wall with

216
00:13:17,692 --> 00:13:20,385
a wide stairway running along its top.

217
00:13:20,385 --> 00:13:24,615
The wall was built to keep
sand off a scenic road.

218
00:13:24,615 --> 00:13:29,231
It was never designed as a defense
against a rising ocean, and

219
00:13:29,231 --> 00:13:33,462
it has been repaired, buried,
and dug out many times since.

220
00:13:33,462 --> 00:13:38,462
Walk the beach at low tide and
you can see where the concrete

221
00:13:38,462 --> 00:13:43,077
has been patched in different decades,
each repair a slightly different color.

222
00:13:43,077 --> 00:13:46,154
California's official planning
guidance tells cities to prepare

223
00:13:46,154 --> 00:13:49,231
for sea levels between
roughly one and three

224
00:13:49,231 --> 00:13:52,308
feet higher by the end of this century.

225
00:13:52,308 --> 00:13:54,231
Higher numbers are considered possible.

226
00:13:54,231 --> 00:13:56,923
At Ocean Beach the beach already narrows

227
00:13:56,923 --> 00:13:59,615
to nothing during the
largest winter swells.

228
00:13:59,615 --> 00:14:06,154
Waves reach the base of the wall and throw
spray over the top onto the old lanes.

229
00:14:06,154 --> 00:14:10,385
Add more water and the same
storms reach it more often.

230
00:14:10,385 --> 00:14:16,923
Nothing on the west side of the city is
built to a higher standard than that wall.

231
00:14:16,923 --> 00:14:20,000
California law already
requires sellers to hand buyers

232
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,077
a natural hazard disclosure
before a sale closes.

233
00:14:23,077 --> 00:14:26,538
It lists flood zones, fire
zones, and earthquake faults.

234
00:14:26,538 --> 00:14:30,769
Coastal erosion is a newer
entry on that form, and it

235
00:14:30,769 --> 00:14:35,000
does not say how many winters
a given seawall has left.

236
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:36,923
Insurance follows the same pattern.

237
00:14:36,923 --> 00:14:41,538
A standard homeowner policy covers
wind and fire, and flood coverage from

238
00:14:41,538 --> 00:14:45,769
the sea is a separate federal
policy with its own limits.

239
00:14:45,769 --> 00:14:48,462
Several owners along
the coast have learned

240
00:14:48,462 --> 00:14:51,154
that distinction only
after the water arrived.

241
00:14:51,154 --> 00:14:56,154
On the Great Highway row, the house
may be worth three million dollars.

242
00:14:56,154 --> 00:14:59,615
The coverage behind it
is a fraction of that.

243
00:14:59,615 --> 00:15:02,692
The third problem is written into the law.

244
00:15:02,692 --> 00:15:05,769
The Coastal Commission has
become steadily less willing

245
00:15:05,769 --> 00:15:08,846
to approve new hard armoring
anywhere in California.

246
00:15:08,846 --> 00:15:12,692
A wall that protects a
house or a road destroys

247
00:15:12,692 --> 00:15:16,154
the public beach in front of it over time.

248
00:15:16,154 --> 00:15:18,462
The commission's job is the beach.

249
00:15:18,462 --> 00:15:23,462
San Francisco got its rock revetment
south of Sloat only as an emergency.

250
00:15:23,462 --> 00:15:29,615
The price of that permission was a promise
to remove the road and rebuild the beach.

251
00:15:29,615 --> 00:15:33,846
Suppose the owner of a three
million dollar house on the

252
00:15:33,846 --> 00:15:37,692
highway wanted a new wall
in front of the block.

253
00:15:37,692 --> 00:15:43,462
They would be asking the same agency
that just told their own city to retreat.

254
00:15:43,462 --> 00:15:48,462
There is no realistic version of
that request that ends with a permit.

255
00:15:48,462 --> 00:15:52,308
Put those three problems
together and you can see what

256
00:15:52,308 --> 00:15:55,769
the highway actually was
for the people behind it.

257
00:15:55,769 --> 00:16:00,000
It was a buffer between their
front doors and the ocean.

258
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:03,077
A hundred feet of pavement and plowed sand

259
00:16:03,077 --> 00:16:05,769
absorbed whatever came
off the water first.

260
00:16:05,769 --> 00:16:11,923
That buffer is now a park in the middle
and a demolition site in the south.

261
00:16:11,923 --> 00:16:16,154
Nobody has proposed knocking down
the houses, and nobody needs to.

262
00:16:16,154 --> 00:16:19,231
The city's own adaptation
plan draws a line

263
00:16:19,231 --> 00:16:22,308
at the sewer tunnel and
the treatment plant.

264
00:16:22,308 --> 00:16:26,538
The houses along the upper highway
sit outside that line, behind

265
00:16:26,538 --> 00:16:30,385
a wall that the plan does
not promise to replace.

266
00:16:30,385 --> 00:16:35,000
Ask a city engineer what happens
to that wall in fifty years

267
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:39,615
and the honest answer is that
nobody has funded the study yet.

268
00:16:39,615 --> 00:16:44,615
The distance between the sea and the
front door has simply gotten shorter.

269
00:16:44,615 --> 00:16:49,231
Every plan the city has published
assumes it will keep getting shorter.

270
00:16:49,231 --> 00:16:54,231
There is one more piece of the
puzzle that most residents never see.

271
00:16:54,231 --> 00:16:56,923
Sand does not only leave Ocean Beach.

272
00:16:56,923 --> 00:17:00,000
Some of it is put back by machine.

273
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:03,846
The Army Corps of Engineers
regularly dredges the main shipping

274
00:17:03,846 --> 00:17:07,692
channel outside the Golden
Gate to keep the port open.

275
00:17:07,692 --> 00:17:11,538
For years part of that
dredged sand has been placed

276
00:17:11,538 --> 00:17:15,385
offshore of Ocean Beach or
directly on the southern shore.

277
00:17:15,385 --> 00:17:18,077
Barges and pipelines
move it, and bulldozers

278
00:17:18,077 --> 00:17:20,769
spread it along the bluff at Sloat.

279
00:17:20,769 --> 00:17:25,769
It is the same practice used on
beaches in Florida and New Jersey.

280
00:17:25,769 --> 00:17:29,231
It buys time, and it has to be repeated.

281
00:17:29,231 --> 00:17:34,231
A single placement can involve hundreds
of thousands of cubic yards of sand.

282
00:17:34,231 --> 00:17:40,000
Much of it washes away again within a
few storm seasons, and the barges return.

283
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,462
Every load of sand that
arrives is a reminder.

284
00:17:43,462 --> 00:17:46,923
The beach in its natural
state is no longer

285
00:17:46,923 --> 00:17:50,000
wide enough to protect
what sits behind it.

286
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:55,769
To see how fast the ending can come,
look at the neighbors who already lost.

287
00:17:55,769 --> 00:17:59,231
About fifteen miles south
lies the town of Pacifica.

288
00:17:59,231 --> 00:18:03,077
A row of apartment buildings
on Esplanade Avenue stood there

289
00:18:03,077 --> 00:18:06,923
on a bluff very much
like the one at Sloat.

290
00:18:06,923 --> 00:18:10,385
In the winter of 2016 the same kind of

291
00:18:10,385 --> 00:18:13,846
storms that hit Ocean Beach
in 2010 arrived again.

292
00:18:13,846 --> 00:18:18,462
They cut that bluff back until
the balconies hung over open air.

293
00:18:18,462 --> 00:18:20,769
The city declared the
buildings uninhabitable.

294
00:18:20,769 --> 00:18:24,615
Residents were given hours
to carry out what they could.

295
00:18:24,615 --> 00:18:28,846
Excavators tore the structures down
while news helicopters filmed from above.

296
00:18:28,846 --> 00:18:30,769
Those were ordinary rental apartments.

297
00:18:30,769 --> 00:18:34,231
Their owners had no more power to stop the

298
00:18:34,231 --> 00:18:37,308
ocean than anyone on
the Great Highway does.

299
00:18:37,308 --> 00:18:42,692
Some of them had bought only a few
years earlier at full market price.

300
00:18:42,692 --> 00:18:46,154
A bluff that looked solid on the day of

301
00:18:46,154 --> 00:18:49,231
the inspection was gone
within a single winter.

302
00:18:49,231 --> 00:18:53,462
The difference at Ocean Beach
is only that the sewer tunnel

303
00:18:53,462 --> 00:18:57,692
gave the city a reason to act
before the buildings fell.

304
00:18:57,692 --> 00:19:02,308
Go back further and this neighborhood
has done all of this before.

305
00:19:02,308 --> 00:19:07,692
At the north end of the beach the
road climbs toward the Cliff House.

306
00:19:07,692 --> 00:19:12,308
An amusement park called Playland
at the Beach ran there for decades.

307
00:19:12,308 --> 00:19:16,154
It had a wooden roller
coaster, a funhouse with a

308
00:19:16,154 --> 00:19:19,615
laughing mechanical woman
at the door, and a carousel.

309
00:19:19,615 --> 00:19:23,077
It closed in 1972 and was
replaced by condominiums.

310
00:19:23,077 --> 00:19:26,538
Just up the hill, the
Sutro Baths were once

311
00:19:26,538 --> 00:19:29,615
the largest indoor swimming
complex in the world.

312
00:19:29,615 --> 00:19:33,077
They burned in 1966 and
were left as concrete

313
00:19:33,077 --> 00:19:36,154
ruins that fill with
seawater at high tide.

314
00:19:36,154 --> 00:19:40,385
At the south end, the Fleishhacker
Pool beside the zoo was

315
00:19:40,385 --> 00:19:44,615
for years the largest outdoor
swimming pool in the United States.

316
00:19:44,615 --> 00:19:48,462
It was filled with water
pumped straight from the ocean.

317
00:19:48,462 --> 00:19:52,308
It closed in 1971 after storms
damaged the intake pipe,

318
00:19:52,308 --> 00:19:55,769
and the empty basin was
eventually filled with dirt.

319
00:19:55,769 --> 00:19:58,846
Every generation at Ocean
Beach has built something

320
00:19:58,846 --> 00:20:01,538
ambitious at the edge of the water.

321
00:20:01,538 --> 00:20:04,615
Every generation has then
watched the edge move.

322
00:20:04,615 --> 00:20:08,462
What is different this
time is that the city wrote

323
00:20:08,462 --> 00:20:12,308
the retreat into an official
plan and paid for it.

324
00:20:12,308 --> 00:20:16,923
That is the honest answer to the
question this story started with.

325
00:20:16,923 --> 00:20:20,385
San Francisco is losing
the highway to a single

326
00:20:20,385 --> 00:20:23,846
storm or a single election
only on the surface.

327
00:20:23,846 --> 00:20:27,308
Underneath, it is giving
the road up in stages.

328
00:20:27,308 --> 00:20:30,769
A sewer tunnel and a
public beach were judged

329
00:20:30,769 --> 00:20:34,231
to be worth more than
four lanes of asphalt.

330
00:20:34,231 --> 00:20:39,615
And the law will not allow anyone
to hold the line with rock forever.

331
00:20:39,615 --> 00:20:44,615
The homeowners along the road are
left with the view they paid for

332
00:20:44,615 --> 00:20:49,231
and a shrinking strip of sand
between it and their living rooms.

333
00:20:49,231 --> 00:20:54,231
You can buy a house on the Great
Highway for three million dollars.

334
00:20:54,231 --> 00:20:58,077
You can stand at your
upstairs window and watch the

335
00:20:58,077 --> 00:21:01,923
entire Pacific Ocean arrive
in front of you every evening.

336
00:21:01,923 --> 00:21:06,154
You can sweep the sand off
your steps every morning and

337
00:21:06,154 --> 00:21:10,000
repaint the salt off your
garage door every few years.

338
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:14,615
But you cannot get the city
to plow the road that used

339
00:21:14,615 --> 00:21:18,846
to catch that sand, because
the road is a park now.

340
00:21:18,846 --> 00:21:23,462
You cannot get the state to
approve a wall, because the state

341
00:21:23,462 --> 00:21:28,077
just spent a decade convincing
your own city to take one down.

342
00:21:28,077 --> 00:21:31,154
And you cannot make the beach stay put.

343
00:21:31,154 --> 00:21:36,154
The sand at Ocean Beach has been
moving since before the seawall, before

344
00:21:36,154 --> 00:21:40,769
the highway, and before anyone
drew a lot line in the dunes.

345
00:21:40,769 --> 00:21:43,846
Which leaves the question
that every owner on

346
00:21:43,846 --> 00:21:46,538
that row will eventually have to answer.

347
00:21:46,538 --> 00:21:51,154
If the city was right to pull
its own highway back from

348
00:21:51,154 --> 00:21:55,769
the water, what does that say about
the houses across the pavement?

349
00:21:55,769 --> 00:21:59,231
Tell me in the comments what you think San

350
00:21:59,231 --> 00:22:02,308
Francisco should do with
the next hundred feet.

351
00:22:02,308 --> 00:22:05,769
Tell me whether you
would sign a thirty year

352
00:22:05,769 --> 00:22:09,231
loan on a house that
faces the open Pacific.

353
00:22:09,231 --> 00:22:12,692
I read every comment on these, and on this

354
00:22:12,692 --> 00:22:16,154
one I genuinely want
to know where you land.
